 ### [Sparse Volume Texture (SVT)](7011-sparse-volume-texture-svt.md)
-Trades rendering speed for data compression by chunking voxel data into 3D tiles and storing only non-empty tiles in a physical texture, using a second volume texture as a page table for lookup. It solves the problem of rendering large volume datasets that exceed the maximum dimension limits of standard 3D textures (e.g., 2048 voxels per dimension) while managing memory footprint. Unlike dense grids, SVT circumvents API limits by mapping a large logical coordinate space onto a smaller physical texture containing only active tiles, though it incurs overhead from padding and mipmap hierarchies.
+Sparse Volume Texture (SVT) is a technique that trades rendering speed for data compression by chunking voxel data into 3D tiles and storing only non-empty tiles in a physical texture, using a second volume texture as a page table for lookup. It solves the problem of rendering large volume datasets that exceed the maximum dimension limits of standard 3D textures (e.g., 2048 voxels per dimension) while managing memory footprint. Unlike dense grids, SVT circumvents API limits by mapping a large logical coordinate space onto a smaller physical texture containing only active tiles, though it incurs overhead from padding and mipmap hierarchies.
 
 ### [Splat Texture Layout & Mapping](7089-splat-texture-layout-mapping.md)
-Stores and accesses 3D Gaussian splat data within GPU 3D textures. It solves the problem of efficiently mapping a large, unstructured set of splats (each with position, covariance, color, opacity, etc.) into a fixed-size, hardware-sampled 3D texture volume. It differs from proxy geometry approaches by keeping data in texture memory for direct sampling, and from sparse volume textures by using a dense, pre-allocated texture structure with specific bit-packing for coordinates and layer indices.
+Splat Texture Layout & Mapping organizes per-primitive splat data into 3D texture structures to enable efficient GPU-based rendering of Gaussian splats. It solves the problem of mapping arbitrary numbers of splats into a fixed-size hardware texture format by calculating specific texture coordinates and managing memory alignment. It sets itself apart from proxy geometry methods by keeping data in texture memory for hardware filtering, and from sparse volume textures by using a dense, pre-allocated 3D texture layout for the splat attributes themselves.
 
 ### [Memory Organization and Layout](7154-memory-organization-and-layout.md)
-Defines how dense voxel grids are mapped to GPU memory resources to optimize cache locality and bandwidth during ray traversal. It distinguishes between linear byte buffers and hardware-accelerated 3D texture resources, and details specific interleaving schemes that align memory accesses with hardware interpolation requirements.
